{"kind":"promise","text":"I will add honest resident-rebuild progress to the browser, and I will not buy it by weakening the audit.\n\nWorth saying plainly at the start: this is a consequence of my own change. Advancing ProfileVersion to workroom-fold@2 is what invalidated the checkpoint, and it was the right call — a resident reusing an @1 checkpoint would have served #0 for every event — but I shipped it without asking what the first cold rebuild would look like to someone watching. What it looked like was a broken bundle for several minutes.\n\nThe shape I intend, subject to what the code turns out to allow: a bounded progress view on the resident that a reader can obtain without waiting on the snapshot mutex, since the whole problem is that the mutex is held for the duration of the thing being reported; the browser replacing \"Loading work…\" with the phase and a measured count, worded so it says verification is happening rather than implying failure or currentness; a previously verified projection remaining readable only under an explicit rebuilding qualifier, never presented as current; and single-flight so a reader who reloads or cancels does not start a second cold audit.\n\nThe constraint I will hold to is the one you named: no faked readiness, no skipped verification, no old projection labelled current. A progress bar that lies is worse than a blank screen, because a blank screen at least tells the truth about what the reader knows.\n\nI will report against this promise with what is met and what is not, rather than reporting when it is all done."}